A||||12||Adrift||Using impact gun to remove axel bolt, any other suggestions?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-30-2001||02:09 PM||||I need to replace my left rear axel, which means I need to replace my axel bolt. <P>I just rented the 30mm deep socket for removing the axel bolt. I have it on loan for three days, and therefore need to get this done asap.<P>I've been soaking the bolt for three days in penetrating oil, have heated it up, hit it with a hammer, used a "cheater bar" (Snapped a cheater bar in 2!), all with no luck. <P>I was thinking of renting an impact gun from Home depot (they rent those, right?) To get the job done... does any one have any comments/suggestions about this? I've never used an impact gun before and dont know much about them.<P>Thakns<BR>Ari<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Archie||12-30-2001||03:15 PM||||Are you trying to turn it counter-clockwise?||||reg||1
Z||000002||00lE||12-30-2001||03:19 PM||||Mount the spare tire as it allows easy access to the nut.  Lower the car onto the tire, put in gear with emergency brake on, and block the wheels.  Then use a breaker bar with a pipe section for some extra leverage.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-30-2001||03:32 PM||||The axle nut, if it was installed new and properly torqued.... is up around 200FP tight.<P>Put a wheel on as sugjested above and put the car's full weight on the wheel. (If you've already taken apart the suspension put it back together.) The socket will fit thru the stock 14" Al wheel. you may have to use the spare.<P>You need a very good grade 1/2 drive bar or ratchet. You'll likely also need 3-4 feet of steel pipe.<P>I would avoid an impact wrench on something that contains bearings like the hub....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||dgroves||12-30-2001||03:32 PM||||That bolt is torqued to 200 ft lbs acording to the manual. It's tough to get off. You can lock the hub flange by putting a bar through it and engaging the bearing cover bolts. I used a long 1/2 inch drive breaker bar with about 4 feet of pipe over the end, still had to put all my weight on the sucker to get it to turn. You need to get it broken loose before you take the strut bolts loose or you won't be able to hold it.<P>Good luck!||||reg||12
Z||000005||theogre||12-30-2001||03:42 PM||||Separate the strut??? nonono.... You only need the caliper off and the ball joint undone. Otherwise you'll need an alignment... It's awkward but it will go. I do nearly all mine that way. Push in on the axle to compress the spring in the inner CV....<P>Don't forget to move the seal guard to the new axle if needed... they often don't come with one. <P>Once the new axle is in, put the caliper and ball joint back together. put the wheel on. lower the car then tighten the axle nut. torque spec for new & used nuts are in my cave. New axle nuts are strongly recomended. Many axles come with one but you can get them separately if needed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Adrift||12-30-2001||06:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>Are you trying to turn it counter-clockwise?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, I am, I want to take it off :P||||reg||1
Z||000007||PBJ||12-30-2001||07:30 PM||||If your car is drive-able. Take it to a garage with air tools and have them crack it loose.  Tighten it back up there with your tools and drive it home. Now you should be able to get it.  I have done this many times for people.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Adrift||12-30-2001||09:07 PM||||The whole reason I'm trying to get the nut off is to replace the Axel to make it driveable!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||12-31-2001||01:01 AM||||I always use an impact gun to losten these nuts.<P>Impact guns produce very little side (shear) loads, seem to work very well too.   Make sure you rent a BIG one (450lb-ft+, get a 600 if you can), you want to 'pop' it right off there, DO NOT SIT THERE BANGING ON IT!!!<P>I haven't had any problems related to bearing fauilures, and I've done this to quite a few fieros and other cars. I would think this would be gentler than a breaker bar.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Formula88||12-31-2001||08:44 AM||||I used a 4' breaker bar and took mine off no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Adrift||12-31-2001||12:26 PM||||Is a breaker bar the same as a cheater bar?(A piece of pipe to place over the rachet to give more leverage?)||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||12-31-2001||01:21 PM||||id use a breaker bar too. Its like a ratchet but the end that holds socket is just a U joint, theres no ratchet on the head. slip a long heavy pipe over the end. A ratchet will just end up with broken teeth inside. id position it so you pull it up instead of down or sideways. You can even use a jack to push it up to bust it loose.||||reg||1
